=== 1000
The snow has barely melted from the churchyard at Carleon when the news runs through every inn and hall in Logres: the boy who drew the sword from the stone has been crowned. His name is Arthur. He is fifteen years old, his beard has not yet come in, and eleven kings have already sworn they will not kneel to him.

At Pentecost the young king holds his first great feast. The hall is half built and the roof lets in the rain, but the tables groan. An old man in a wet grey cloak sits at the king's elbow and eats nothing. "That is Merlin," a squire whispers to you, "and he knew the king would win before the king did."

When the meat is cleared, Arthur climbs onto his own table so that everyone can see him. "I will not ask you to love me," he says. "I am too young to be loved. I will ask you to swear. Never to do outrage nor murder. To give mercy to any who ask it. To help ladies, damsels and widows in their need. Never to fight in a wrongful quarrel for any law or any gold." He looks down the long hall, from the lords at the high table to the knights crowded by the door. "Who will swear with me?"

King Lot of Orkney's herald does not wait to hear the answers. He is already on his horse.

Each Knight chooses how to answer the young king:

Those who kneel and swear the oath before the Bishop of Canterbury, meaning every word, gain 1 Rank of Divinity.

Those who swear it loudly, with an eye on the queen's ladies watching from the gallery, gain 1 Rank of Romance.

Those who swear it with their fingers crossed beneath the table, and slip out afterwards to find King Lot's herald in the stable yard, gain 1 Rank of Villainy.
[n:The Knight with the fewest Ranks of Renown may draw 1 Quest Card]

=== 2000
Years have passed. Arthur's beard is thick and there is grey in it. The Round Table is full but for one seat, which has stood empty since the day it was made. Gold letters on its back read *the Siege Perilous*, and no one who has sat in it has lived to stand up again.

It is Pentecost once more. The hall at Camelot is full, and the talk is of tournaments and marriages, when every door and shutter in the castle slams at once. A thunderclap shakes the roof. Then a light comes in, brighter than the sun on water, and every face in the hall looks younger in it. Something covered in white samite passes down the length of the tables. No one carries it. Wherever it goes, each knight finds before them the meat and drink they love best. Then it is gone, and the doors creak open on an ordinary evening.

For a long moment nobody speaks. Then Sir Bors stands up so fast that his bench goes over. "I saw it covered," he says, "and I will not rest until I see it bare. I swear to seek it for a year and a day, and longer if God wills." One by one, the knights of the Round Table rise and swear with him.

Arthur does not rise. "You have broken my fellowship," he says quietly, "and I will never see all of you at this table again."

By morning, the rivers in the west have begun to run low, and word comes that the fields around Corbenic have turned to dust.

Each Knight chooses how to answer the Grail's call:

Those who swear to seek the Grail and its mysteries, whatever it costs them, gain 1 Rank of Divinity.

Those who stay to protect the people and the ladies they love while the fellowship scatters, gain 1 Rank of Romance.

Those who say aloud what several at the table are thinking, that a court without its best knights is a court for the taking, gain 1 Rank of Villainy.
[n:Every Knight gains Destiny equal to their highest Rank of Renown | n:Every Knight who is Betrothed and has 0 Ranks of Villainy gains 1 Rank of Romance]

=== 3000
The Grail is gone from Britain, and more than half the knights who went looking for it never came home. Those who did are changed. They pray more and laugh less, and they watch one another.

On a grey May morning, Sir Agravain and Sir Mordred stand before the king in his own hall and accuse the queen of treason. Agravain reads the charges from a list he has kept for years. Mordred only says, softly, "I am sorry, Father. Someone had to tell you." The law is the law, and Arthur made it. Guinevere is sentenced to burn.

On the day of the burning, the queen's champion of the house of Benwick and his kinsmen cut their way through the guard around the stake and carry her away. In the crush, two unarmed knights of Orkney are struck down. They are Sir Gareth, whom everyone loved, and his brother Sir Gaheris. The house of Orkney swears it will have blood for blood. Arthur, grey and heavy now, takes ship with his host to besiege Benwick across the sea, and leaves Mordred to rule Britain in his absence.

Within the month, letters are read in every market cross saying the king is dead in France. Mordred has had himself crowned at Canterbury. Saxon ships are beaching on the eastern shore, and the men who crew them say they were invited.

Each Knight chooses where their loyalty lies:

Those who swear to hold the realm for Arthur and restore Camelot's glory gain 1 Rank of Divinity.

Those who ride to protect what good has already been done, the villages, the abbeys and the people they love, gain 1 Rank of Romance.

Those who ride to Canterbury to kneel to King Mordred gain 1 Rank of Villainy.
[n:Every Knight gains Destiny equal to their highest Rank of Renown | n:Every Knight with 3 or more Ranks of Villainy may gain 1 Treasure]

=== 9999
The last battle is over. On Salisbury Plain the dead of both hosts lie together in the rain, and no herald can tell you any longer who fought for whom. Mordred is dead by his father's spear. Arthur has been carried to the water's edge, where a black barge waited with three queens in it, and none of those who watched it go into the mist can agree on what they saw.

Every Knight adds Destiny equal to their total Ranks of Renown. The Knight with the most Destiny is remembered longest. Then the table counts which kind of Renown all the Knights together have gathered most, and reads the ending that matches it.

**If Divinity is greatest:** Word spreads among the abbeys that the king is not dead but sleeping, and that he will wake when Britain has most need of him. The knights who survive give away their horses and take up the habit. For a hundred years the chapels on the old roads are kept by men and women who were once feared in the lists, and travellers are never turned away from them.

**If Romance is greatest:** The songs begin before the grass has grown back on the plain. Minstrels carry them to every court in Christendom, and the deeds grow larger with every telling. Ladies who were never at Camelot swear they danced there. In a hundred halls, young knights who were not yet born when the Round Table broke kneel before a king who is not Arthur and swear an oath they learned from a song.

**If Villainy is greatest:** The Saxon kings divide Logres like a joint of meat, and those knights who helped them take their share. The castles are handed to new masters. The Round Table itself is chopped up for firewood by a steward who does not know what it is. Somewhere, perhaps, a sword lies at the bottom of a lake. Nobody goes to look for it.
